Authorization is expressed by installed participants and participant-scoped
`GrantBinding` records. This page keeps its historical URL so existing links do
not break; the retired deployment-authority proposal/materialization model no
longer exists.

## Installed participant revisions

`Auth.Participants.Install` stores immutable verified source-package evidence and
the exact participant lexical path. Installing a participant defines what may be granted; it
does not grant anything by itself.

## Identity grants

`Auth.Grants.Set` writes one binding for an identity and participant. The
binding selects an installed revision, approval mode, approved capability consent
fingerprints, resource commitments, delegation ceiling, exact derived grants,
and finite platform privileges. Writes require `expectedRevision`.

Effective application authority is:

```text
selected participant actions
  intersect approved current-consent capabilities
  intersect delegation ceilings
  intersect usable approved resource commitments
```

NATS subjects, API packages, resource names, and client-provided artifacts never
add authority.

## Deployments and resources

`Auth.Deployments.Apply` is the direct deployment mutation. It validates the
installed participant and creates/adopts declared resource bindings. Required
missing evidence prevents context issuance; unavailable optional evidence
removes only affected optional grants.

Services receive typed resource handles from `TrellisService.connect(...)`.
They do not fetch raw bindings or derive physical names.

`Auth.ServiceInstances.Provision` commits the immutable caller-supplied public key
and awaits the same native resource producer used by runtime startup before
returning success. Empty declaration sets and native KV are supported; other
resource declarations currently return an explicit failure rather than empty
bindings. Retry the same provisioning request after a broker failure to resume
materialization without replacing the instance or destroying retained data.

Omitting a resource in a deployment version, disabling a deployment, or removing
the deployment detaches its resources. It does **not** delete their allocations
or data. `Auth.Resources.List` includes detached resources; filter by `ownerKind`
and `ownerId` when needed. Resource IDs are digests, and each row exposes its
actual lifecycle `state`, participant, local name, kind, and revision.

A compatible approved re-add reuses the retained allocation and its data. Changes
that reduce KV history or TTL retention are rejected instead of recreating the
bucket. An administrator may explicitly choose `Auth.Resources.Remove` for a
detached KV resource, supplying its current revision. Active resources cannot be
deleted. Trellis checks the physical ownership marker before deleting only the
selected allocation. If the broker call fails, retry the same request to resume
that manual choice; startup never chooses or resumes deletion automatically.

## Administration

The `Admin` platform privilege gates Auth administrative RPCs. It is carried in
the current participant-scoped binding and does not imply all application
permissions. First-admin setup atomically grants Admin for the built-in CLI and
Console participants; the activation Portal receives no Admin binding.

The CLI uses `participants install`, `identity grants get/list/set/revoke`,
`issuers list/rotate/revoke`, and direct `svc`/`dev` apply/get commands. Console
shows installed revisions, current bindings, deployments, resources, logins,
connections, and issuer state. There are no plans, proposals, acceptance, or
reconcile actions.

## Runtime issuance

Auth rereads current identity/login, binding, installed revision, resources, and
issuer inside the signing transaction. It stores the exact signed context and
issuance snapshot by digest, then returns server-owned runtime and transport
data. Auth Callout repeats the current-state comparison before issuing a
short-lived NATS JWT.

Every mutation and idempotency replay is authorized in the same SQL transaction
against the actor's current binding revision and Admin privilege.
